    "practice": "What is an SLO?|A measurable reliability target|A guarantee every task succeeds|A database key|A retry attempt|An SLO defines a measurable target over a population and window.|An SLO is a measurable target rather than a promise of no failures.|A database key defines identity rather than service reliability.|An attempt is an execution event rather than a reliability target.\nWhich Airflow version introduced Deadline Alerts in the cited source?|3.1|1.0|2.2|3.0|The pinned 3.1.0 source documents Deadline Alerts, while legacy SLAs were removed in 3.0.|The pinned documentation introduces Deadline Alerts in 3.1 rather than 1.0.|Version 2.2 predates the documented Deadline Alerts introduction.|Version 3.0 removed legacy SLAs but the cited new feature arrived in 3.1.\nWhat must an elapsed-time deadline specify?|Reference point and interval|Only the maximum retry count|Only the task execution timeout|Only the latest success timestamp|A duration has operational meaning only relative to an explicit origin.|Attempt count does not identify when the elapsed deadline begins.|A timeout for one task does not define the alert\u2019s elapsed reference point.|An observed success time does not define the allowed interval from the chosen origin.\nA run is green at noon but its output was due at 07:00. What happened?|It missed the business deadline|It met freshness because it is green|It necessarily lost all rows|Its logical date changed to noon|Task success does not imply on-time consumer availability.|Green execution at noon still misses the explicit 07:00 obligation.|Lateness alone does not prove all business rows were lost.|Delayed completion does not change the run\u2019s assigned logical date.\nA queue-time alert fires within budget but the source data is two days stale. What is missing?|Business-data freshness|More successful task notifications|Only a shorter retry delay|Only a worker-start timestamp|Queue elapsed time does not establish the age or completeness of underlying data.|Execution notifications do not compare relevant event age with the business promise.|Shorter retries cannot establish freshness of old source events.|A recent worker can process stale business data.\nFour expected runs include three on-time and one missing. If missing is a miss, what rate applies?|75%|100%|25%|33%|The missing expected run remains in the denominator, yielding three out of four.|One missing obligation is a miss, so the result cannot be 100%.|Twenty-five percent is the missed fraction rather than the on-time fraction.|Three divided by four is 75%, not 33%.\nA target allows one missed run per hundred and two are missing. If all others are on time, what rate applies?|98%|99%|100%|2%|Ninety-eight timely outcomes out of one hundred expected obligations yield 98%.|Ninety-nine percent would allow only one missed run, but two are missing.|Missing obligations prevent a perfect success fraction.|Two percent is the miss rate rather than the timely completion rate.",
    "formal": "What is the legacy Airflow SLA status in version 3.1.0?|SLA removed; Deadline Alerts added|Still the unchanged preferred task API|A guarantee of contractual compensation|Identical to a database transaction timeout|The pinned task documentation states removal in 3.0 and replacement in 3.1.|The legacy SLA API was removed in Airflow 3.0 rather than remaining unchanged.|The alert mechanism does not itself define legal compensation obligations.|A DAG deadline alert differs from a database statement timeout.\nWhich alert field makes ownership actionable?|Owning team and runbook|Only the retrying task id|Only the deadline timestamp|Only the current attempt number|An actionable alert identifies a responder and a decision path.|A task id helps location but does not identify the accountable team or response procedure.|The timestamp identifies lateness without assigning an owner or next action.|Attempt count does not tell the recipient who should respond or how.\nWhat does an on-time fraction measure?|On-time share of obligations|The p99 duration directly|The number of graph edges|The error cost in currency automatically|A fraction counts timely outcomes in a defined population; latency percentiles and costs differ.|An on-time proportion is not a percentile of elapsed durations.|Graph size does not measure completion against deadlines.|Timeliness does not convert automatically to monetary impact.\nAn operations alert says only late. Which additional context is most useful?|Interval, asset, due time, state|Only the last retry exception|Only the oldest failure message|Only the scheduler health signal|Context lets responders assess impact and locate the failing obligation.|A stack trace omits the affected business interval and required readiness time.|An old failure may not describe the current state or affected asset.|A healthy scheduler does not establish the affected output\u2019s readiness.\nA team measures only successfully completed runs and reports perfect timeliness despite two missing runs. What is the defect?|A survivor-only denominator|A too-short task timeout|A duplicated run numerator|A late-alert routing failure|Expected but missing obligations must be handled explicitly instead of disappearing from measurement.|A timeout policy does not explain removing missing obligations from the denominator.|The stated flaw is omitted missing runs, rather than counting successes twice.|Alert routing does not define the population used in the reported fraction.\nA customer agreement promises 06:00 readiness, while an internal objective targets 05:45. What is the distinction?|Operating margin before the SLA|Both timestamps must be identical|An SLO replaces all contractual duties|A task retry changes the agreement|Internal objectives can give a buffer while contractual obligations remain separate.|An internal target can intentionally precede an external agreement.|An internal objective does not cancel contractual obligations.|Retry timing does not renegotiate the customer\u2019s deadline.\nNineteen of twenty expected intervals are on time. What is the observed rate?|95%|99%|90%|5%|Nineteen on-time runs divided by twenty expected runs gives 0.95, or 95 percent.|99% would require a different count or denominator.|90% would correspond to eighteen of twenty.|5% is the miss rate, not the on-time rate.\nA business deadline is interval end plus 60 minutes. A run queues 50 minutes after end and finishes 20 minutes later. What is its business result?|10 minutes late|20 minutes late|10 minutes early|On time because queue elapsed is 20|Finish is end+70, exceeding end+60 by ten.|Twenty is elapsed since queue, not lateness beyond deadline.|Ten early would require completion at end+50.|Queue-relative elapsed does not replace the interval-relative obligation.\nA 99% on-time target covers 200 expected runs. With three misses, which statement is correct?|98.5% is below target|99% exactly meets target|99.5% exceeds target|1.5% is the on-time rate|197/200=98.5%, below the 99% objective.|A 99% target permits at most two misses in 200.|99.5% would mean one miss.|1.5% is the miss fraction.\nA Deadline Alert uses a documented queue-time reference and a 30-minute interval. A run queues at 06:50; business output is due at 07:00. Which design meets both monitoring needs?|Separate 07:00 freshness monitoring|Treat 07:20 queue deadline as the business deadline|Remove business monitoring because an alert exists|Assume output is fresh whenever queue delay is small|The queue reference reaches 07:20 and does not encode the 07:00 consumer promise.|07:20 is twenty minutes later than the stated promise.|Existence of an alert does not establish its relevance to business time.|Output freshness needs data evidence as well as queue elapsed time.",
    "problem": "Calculate on-time outcomes against a synthetic minute-of-day deadline.",
